---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'session', label: 'Open a session' },
   { id: 'move', label: 'Move and read' },
   { id: 'structure', label: 'Jump by structure' },
   { id: 'page', label: 'Read the whole page' },
   { id: 'check', label: 'Check what was said' },
   { id: 'batch', label: 'Script a flow' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Named commands' },
   { id: 'transcript', label: 'Checkpoints and transcripts' },
   { id: 'form', label: 'Worked example' },
   { id: 'cleanup', label: 'Cleanup' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test with a screen reader"
   summary="Open a driver session, move through the page the way a screen reader user does, and check what the reader announced. The same commands work against VoiceOver, NVDA, and the virtual reader. Only the screen reader changes."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="session">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 1</p>
            <h2>Open a session</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>sr start</code> takes the page as a positional argument and the screen reader as
            <code>--sr</code>. For VoiceOver or NVDA, a11ied opens the URL in a browser, waits until
            that window is in front, and starts the reader. <code>--browser chrome</code>,
            <code>edge</code>, <code>brave</code>, <code>chromium</code>, or an app name such as
            <code>Safari</code> picks the browser. Without it, a11ied uses the system automation
            browser.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr start --sr voiceover --browser Safari http://localhost:3000/checkout</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>--app &lt;name&gt;</code> reads a native app that is already open instead of a
            page. The session focuses the app by name and waits for it to come to the front.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover --app Calendar</code></pre>
         <p>
            For the virtual reader, a11ied loads the URL in a headless Chromium page and runs the
            reader inside it. See <a href="/targets#virtual">targets</a> for when it falls back to
            jsdom.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            One session is active at a time. Every later <code>sr</code> command uses it, and no
            command takes a session id. A second <code>sr start</code> stops the first session and
            reports it in the warnings. When the window never came to the front, the result carries a
            <code>window-focus-unconfirmed</code> warning that names what is in front instead.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">The virtual reader</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            With the <code>browser</code> engine the page's scripts run, so a live region that a
            script fills, or a dialog a click opens, is announced. With the <code>jsdom</code>
            engine, used when no Chromium is installed, only what is in the markup is announced.
            <code>a1 sr status</code> names the engine.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="move">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 2</p>
         <h2>Move through the page and read the output</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <div class="route-terms"><code>sr next</code>, <code>sr previous</code></div>
            <span>Move one item and return what the reader said about it.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <div class="route-terms"><code>sr top</code>, <code>sr bottom</code></div>
            <span>Move to the start or the end of the document or the current area.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <div class="route-terms"><code>sr interact</code>, <code>sr stop-interacting</code>, <code>sr activate</code></div>
            <span>Enter a group or control, leave it, and activate the current item.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr escape</code>
            <span>Press Escape to dismiss a menu or a dialog.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr read</code>
            <span>The current item without moving: role, name, value, states, the phrase, and where each field came from.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr title</code>
            <span>The page title: <code>document.title</code> on virtual, the window summary (VO-F2) on VoiceOver, the window title (NVDA-T) on NVDA.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr find "Learn more"</code>
            <span>Move the cursor to the next place the text appears. Exits 4 when it is not on the page. Needs an active session.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr press Tab Tab</code>
            <span>Press key chords in order, one chord per argument.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr type "test@example.com"</code>
            <span>Type into the focused control.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>sr open &lt;url&gt;</code>
            <span>Move the running session to another page.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Every response carries a <code>state</code> object with <code>lastSpokenPhrase</code>,
         <code>currentItemText</code>, <code>currentItem</code>, the running
         <code>spokenPhraseLog</code>, and the timestamped <code>transcript</code>.
         <code>currentItem</code> holds the role, name, value, states, and heading level. On the
         virtual reader it comes from the DOM node under the cursor. On VoiceOver the role and
         states are parsed from the <code>name, role</code> phrase and the name is the item text.
         On NVDA all three are parsed from the phrase. The <code>source</code> field says which.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr read  virtual
  Phrase:  checkbox, Accept the terms, not checked
  Item:    Accept the terms
  Role:    checkbox
  Name:    Accept the terms
  States:  unchecked
  Source:  virtual: role and name from the active node (dom-accessibility-api), states from its attributes</code></pre>
      <p>
         Add <code>--phrase</code> to print the phrase alone, one line, for shell loops. Add
         <code>--timeout &lt;ms&gt;</code> to bound how long the screen reader command may take.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="structure">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 3</p>
            <h2>Jump by structure</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Screen reader users do not read pages one item at a time. They jump by heading, link,
            or landmark. <code>sr next &lt;kind&gt;</code> and <code>sr previous &lt;kind&gt;</code>
            do the same on every target. The kinds are <code>heading</code>, <code>link</code>,
            <code>landmark</code>, <code>control</code>, <code>button</code>, <code>table</code>,
            <code>list</code>, <code>graphic</code>, <code>region</code>, and
            <code>form-field</code>.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr next heading
a1 sr next heading --level 2
a1 sr previous link
a1 sr next control --times 3</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>sr elements &lt;kind&gt;</code> is the rotor: it moves to the top and lists every
            element of one kind as the reader announces it. It stops at the end of the page or at
            <code>--max</code>, which defaults to 200, and says which. <code>elements</code>,
            <code>read-all</code>, <code>goto</code>, and <code>find</code> need an active
            session and exit with <code>missing-session</code> without one. Run
            <code>sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual &lt;url&gt;</code> first for the simulated
            reader, since a plain <code>sr start</code> falls back to VoiceOver on macOS.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr elements heading  virtual
4 heading elements from the top of the page.

1. heading level 1: Structure page  heading, Structure page, level 1
2. heading level 2: Products  heading, Products, level 2
3. heading level 3: Pricing  heading, Pricing, level 3
4. heading level 2: Sign up  heading, Sign up, level 2

Stopped at the end of the document.</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>sr goto --role link --name "Learn more"</code> steps forward until the current item
            has that role, that name, or both, and exits 4 when nothing matches before the end.
         </p>
         <p>
            Inside a table, <code>sr table next-cell</code>, <code>previous-cell</code>,
            <code>next-row</code>, <code>previous-row</code>, <code>next-column</code>, and
            <code>previous-column</code> move between cells. <code>sr table column-header</code> and
            <code>row-header</code> read the headers of the current cell. VoiceOver moves with
            VO-arrows inside an interacted table and reads the column header with VO-C. NVDA moves
            with Control-Alt-arrows and speaks headers as the cursor enters a cell, so the header
            reads exit 2 there with that explanation.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">One table, three targets</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Each kind maps to Guidepup's jump method or a VoiceOver Commander phrase, to an NVDA
            single-letter key, and to a <code>virtual.commands.moveToNext*</code> command. The
            virtual reader has no command for button, control, table, list, graphic, and
            form-field, so it walks item by item until the announced role matches, and reports
            <code>moved: false</code> when the page has none.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="page">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 4</p>
         <h2>Read the whole page</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>sr read-all</code> is say-all as a transcript: it steps item by item from the cursor
         to the end of the document, bounded by <code>--max</code> (default 500), and says whether it
         stopped at the end or at the cap. <code>sr walk [url]</code> does the whole thing from cold:
         it starts a session when none is active, opens the URL in the active one otherwise, moves
         to the top, runs read-all, and prints the transcript of that walk. With no session and no
         <code>--sr</code>, <code>walk</code> starts VoiceOver on macOS. Pass
         <code>--sr virtual --allow-virtual</code> for the simulated reader instead.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/checkout --sr virtual --allow-virtual
a1 sr walk --max 40 --out ./evidence/checkout-walk.md</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="check">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 5</p>
            <h2>Check what the reader said</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>sr expect</code> checks the transcript for a phrase and exits 4 when it is not there.
            Plain text matches without regard to case. <code>/pattern/flags</code> is a regular
            expression. <code>--since &lt;checkpoint&gt;</code> only counts phrases after that
            checkpoint, and <code>--not</code> passes when the phrase was not announced.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr expect "Preferences, dialog" --since "opened the dialog"
a1 sr expect /error/i --not</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>sr wait --for &lt;text|/regex/&gt;</code> polls the transcript until a phrase spoken
            after the wait began matches, and exits 4 at <code>--timeout</code> (default 5000 ms).
            It polls the transcript rather than the last phrase, so an announcement that arrives and
            is replaced before the next poll is still caught. Use it for a phrase that arrives on its
            own timeline, such as a live region a network response fills, or the delay before a real
            screen reader finishes speaking. A phrase the triggering command already returned in its
            own response happened before the wait began, so <code>expect</code> on that result is
            the right check instead. Without <code>--for</code>, <code>wait</code> pauses for
            <code>--ms</code>.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr press Enter
a1 sr wait --for /saved/i --timeout 3000</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Exit codes</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>find</code>, <code>goto</code>, <code>wait --for</code>, <code>expect</code>, and a
            <code>batch</code> with a failed expect exit 4. Usage errors, including a command a
            target cannot run or a verb run with no active session, exit 2. A browser or broker
            process that fails to start exits 3.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="batch">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 6</p>
         <h2>Script a flow in one process</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>sr batch [file]</code> reads one action per line, or stdin, and runs them all over
         one broker connection in one process. Each line is the action request the MCP
         <code>sr_action</code> tool takes, or an <code>expect</code> line. Blank lines and lines
         starting with <code>#</code> are skipped. The batch stops at the first failed expect unless
         <code>--continue</code> is set.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr batch &lt;&lt;'EOF'
&#123;"action":"goto","payload":&#123;"role":"button","name":"Open dialog"&#125;&#125;
&#123;"action":"checkpoint","payload":&#123;"label":"opened"&#125;&#125;
&#123;"action":"activate"&#125;
&#123;"action":"expect","payload":&#123;"match":"Preferences","since":"opened"&#125;&#125;
&#123;"action":"escape"&#125;
&#123;"action":"expect","payload":&#123;"match":"Open dialog"&#125;&#125;
EOF</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>sr batch
✓ 1. goto &#123;"role":"button","name":"Open dialog","max":500&#125;  button, Open dialog
✓ 2. checkpoint &#123;"label":"opened"&#125;  button, Open dialog
✓ 3. activate  textbox, Nickname
✓ 4. expect Preferences since "opened"  pass
✓ 5. escape  button, Open dialog
✓ 6. expect Open dialog  pass

6 steps passed.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The checkpoint runs before <code>activate</code>, not after, so <code>expect --since</code>
         sees the dialog's announcement. A phrase already in the triggering action's own response
         happened before a later checkpoint and would not count.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="commands">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 7</p>
            <h2>Run named screen reader commands</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>sr do</code> runs a command by name. The portable set works on every target and
            resolves through the same table the subcommands use: <code>next</code>,
            <code>previous</code>, <code>interact</code>, <code>stop-interacting</code>,
            <code>activate</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>escape</code>, and
            <code>next-&lt;kind&gt;</code> and <code>previous-&lt;kind&gt;</code> for every kind above.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr do interact --json
a1 sr do next-heading --json
a1 sr do stop-interacting --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            VoiceOver and NVDA add their own sets. <code>sr list</code> prints them grouped by command
            set and by what the commands do. Start with <code>--query</code>: the full list is over
            400 lines.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr list --query heading
a1 sr list --query "VO-Command" --sr voiceover</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Command sets</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            When two sets share a name, prefix it with the set:
            <code>sr do voiceover-keycode:move-to-next</code> or
            <code>sr do voiceover-commander:move-right</code>. <code>--command-set</code> picks one
            set for the whole call. The virtual target accepts the portable set only.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="transcript">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 8</p>
         <h2>Mark the steps and keep the transcript</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         A checkpoint marks a point in the transcript. <code>sr transcript --since</code> and
         <code>sr expect --since</code> then look only at what the reader said after it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr checkpoint "opened the dialog"
a1 sr next
a1 sr transcript --since "opened the dialog"</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each entry carries an index, an ISO timestamp, the phrase, and the item text when the
         reader reported one. The Markdown view numbers the phrases 1, 2, 3 and renders a checkpoint
         as a heading between them. <code>--tail &lt;count&gt;</code> keeps the last N phrases.
         <code>--out &lt;path&gt;</code> writes the transcript to a file, as JSON for a
         <code>.json</code> path and as Markdown for a <code>.md</code> path. Pass
         <code>--format json</code> or <code>--format md</code> to pick the format yourself.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr transcript --out ./evidence/checkout.md</code></pre>
      <p>
         On VoiceOver, <code>sr screenshot &lt;path&gt;</code> saves a picture of what the cursor is
         on. NVDA has no cursor screenshot and the virtual reader has no screen, so both exit 2 and
         say so.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="form">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Example</p>
         <h2>Check a form's announcement order</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Step through the form field by field and read the transcript as a sequence. Each entry is
         what the reader said at that position.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"spokenPhraseLog": [
  "document",
  "heading, Checkout, level 1",
  "form",
  "Email",
  "textbox, Email, required",
  "Card number",
  "textbox, Card number",
  "button, Pay now",
  "button",
  "end of form",
  "end of document"
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         Three things to check in a log like this. Every control has a name, so
         <code>"button"</code> on its own is a defect. The order matches the visual order. States
         that matter are announced, which is why <code>required</code> appears on the email field
         and not on the card field.
      </p>
      <p>
         For validation messages, submit the form with <code>sr press Enter</code>, then
         <code>sr wait --for /error/i</code> to see whether the message was announced. Status
         Messages is criterion 4.1.3, and <a href="/coverage">its coverage state</a> is hybrid for
         exactly this reason.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="cleanup">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Cleanup</p>
         <h2>Stop every session you start</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop --out ./evidence/checkout.json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Stopping shuts down the driver process, finishes any recording, and deletes the state
         file. A session left running keeps the screen reader under automation control, so stop
         each one you start. A session nobody has used for 30 minutes stops itself. Pass
         <code>--idle-timeout &lt;minutes&gt;</code> on <code>sr start</code> to change that, or
         <code>--idle-timeout 0</code> to turn it off.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr stop --out</code> writes the transcript on the way out. When the session recorded
         video, a11ied also writes the Markdown transcript next to the recording file, so you can
         read the two together.
      </p>
      <p>
         For a single action with no session, add <code>--ephemeral</code> to
         <code>press</code>, <code>type</code>, or <code>do</code>. It starts a session, runs the
         action, and tears it down in one command.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
